#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ollama-dispatch-slice -- slice a single-file (or small-set) omnibus coding task
into N atomic, INDEPENDENTLY-GATED sub-dispatches, run as a resumable chain.

WHY
---
`ollama-queue.py --auto-split` splits per-target-file and gates the whole chain
ONCE (no per-slice adversarial verify, no per-slice relevance gate). That fails an
omnibus whose ~10 items all live in ONE file (server.py) -- per-target splitting
yields a single slice, and a single end-gate drops the two things that make a
dispatch trustworthy: per-slice adversarial verify + the relevance gate.

This tool slices a single-file omnibus into per-PROPERTY units, gates each on its
OWN adversarial verify (full author -> draft -> preflight PER SLICE), and chains
them so each slice's baseline is the previous slice's LANDED tree.

CHAINING MODEL (chain worktree, commit-between-slices)
------------------------------------------------------
STEP 0 cuts ONE "chain worktree" of the repo, checked out on branch
`slice/<label>` at repo HEAD. Each slice is authored in its OWN worktree cut off
the chain worktree's CURRENT tip (`ollama-dispatch-auto --repo <chain-wt>` /
`ollama-dispatch-scaffold --repo <chain-wt>` -- auto/scaffold cut from that repo's
HEAD, which IS the chain tip), so a dependent slice sees the code its predecessors
landed. When a slice's dispatch CONVERGES, its edited target file is copied into
the chain worktree and committed onto `slice/<label>`, advancing the tip for the
next slice. Slices run strictly sequentially (a slice is enqueued only after the
previous one is DONE and committed), so no queue-level `--after` is needed and
there is no shared-file write contention. At the end the chain branch holds every
converged slice; integrate by copying its tree onto main -- never merge (memory
reference_dispatch_deliverable_uncommitted_in_worktree).

THE RELEVANCE GATE (no human park by default -- 2026-09-18)
-----------------------------------------------------------
Per-slice RELEVANCE (does the verify test the right property, not merely
discriminate?) used to STOP for a human `--confirm-slice`. The owner: "no human
relevance review gate. there's only a claude gate, and even then that should be
minimal since our pipeline should be able to handle the vast majority of stuff."
So `--execute` now AUTO-CONFIRMS an authored slice straight into the MECHANICAL
relevance gate (draft --confirm + preflight both-ways + verify-relevance). On GO
it enqueues; on the rare NO-GO it marks the slice ESCALATED -- surfaced to the
Claude gate (the run-status needs-eyes panel), NOT parked for a human. `--execute`
is still a RESUMABLE state machine that stops at a still-running queue job.
`--manual-review` restores the old human park; manual author mode always parks
(its scaffold case list is deliberately empty).

INPUT: a JSON plan (see prototypes/voicemail-inbox.slices.json):
  { repo, target, lang, label, slices: [ {id, title, intent, depends_on:[ids],
    entry_point, must_contain:[...], verify_shape} ] }

USAGE
-----
  ollama-dispatch-slice <plan.json>                       # dry-run: plan + state
  ollama-dispatch-slice <plan.json> --execute            # advance to next checkpoint
  ollama-dispatch-slice <plan.json> --confirm-slice ID   # mark cases reviewed
  ollama-dispatch-slice <plan.json> --status             # slice states
  ollama-dispatch-slice <plan.json> --reset              # discard run state
"""
import argparse
import errno
import inspect
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys

# GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 (2026-10-02, Rivian s5 566e7cc77629 / 5059223eacc3 / 454979f2a248):
# a plain `git status` REWRITES .git/index (it takes index.lock to refresh stat
# info). Our observers (worktree snapshots, dirty checks) run it with timeouts
# against .git dirs inside the iCloud Desktop repos, where it can be slow -- and a
# held or timeout-orphaned index.lock made auto-harness-check's `git checkout`
# fail with "index.lock: File exists" -> "HARNESS ERROR: git is unusable". With
# this set, read-only commands never take the optional lock; writes still lock.
os.environ.setdefault("GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS", "0")

# iCloud "Optimize Storage" evicts the Desktop repos' .git metadata; without this a
# git child gets EDEADLK ("not a git repository: (null)") -- see dataless_policy.py.
try:
    import importlib.util as _dp_iu, os as _dp_os
    _dp_s = _dp_iu.spec_from_file_location("dataless_policy", _dp_os.path.join(
        _dp_os.path.dirname(_dp_os.path.realpath(__file__)), "dataless_policy.py"))
    _dp_m = _dp_iu.module_from_spec(_dp_s)
    _dp_s.loader.exec_module(_dp_m)
    _dp_m.enable()
except Exception:
    pass
import time
from datetime import datetime, timezone
from pathlib import Path

AUTO = os.path.expanduser("~/bin/ollama-dispatch-auto")
SCAFFOLD = os.path.expanduser("~/bin/ollama-dispatch-scaffold")
DRAFT = os.path.expanduser("~/bin/ollama-dispatch-draft")
